Title: Scaling regression guard — empirical N-vs-2N for corpus-heavy commands
Created: 2026-05-29
Author: Claude

--- log ---
2026-05-29T16:51Z Claude created — follow-up to 0154; adds an empirical end-to-end scaling guard to complement the counter-based op-count guard and the wall-clock backstop

2026-05-29T17:20Z Claude note assertion signal is allocation VOLUME (TotalAlloc bytes), not allocation count (Mallocs): mutation testing showed `append` amortises an O(N^2)-byte build into O(N log N) malloc events, so count misses it but bytes trip ~16x. Compute-bound zero-alloc O(N^2) still slips past — owned by 0154 wall-clock backstop + review (defense-in-depth, accepted on #169)

--- body ---
## Context

0154 shipped two `fast`-invariant guards: a deterministic parse-once /
op-count counter (`src/go/contract_test.go`) and a non-blocking wall-clock
backstop (`tests/test_contract.sh`). Verification of 0154 surfaced a real
limit: the op-count counter is pinned to corpus size by construction, so an
O(N²) *algorithm* inside a per-item loop can pass it silently. The wall-clock
backstop catches catastrophe but not a 2×→4× regression.

This ticket adds the empirical guard the author asked for: measure real
allocation volume (TotalAlloc bytes) and wall-clock time of the corpus-heavy
commands across a geometric ladder of corpus sizes and assert the growth is
linear. Allocation volume is deterministic for fixed input (immune to CI-box
speed), so it is the assertion signal; time is logged for inspection. (Volume,
not count: `append` amortises an O(N²)-byte build into O(N log N) malloc
events, so a count-based check would miss it.)

It is slow (builds large stores, runs six commands) and is a regression guard,
not a per-merge check — so it is build-tagged out of the default suite and run
on demand.

## Relevant files
- `src/go/scaling_test.go` — new, `//go:build scaling`
- `Makefile` — new `test-scaling` target; excluded from `test`
- `src/go/erg.go` `loadErgs`, `src/go/validate.go` `validateCorpus`,
  `detectCycles` — the scanned-once / DAG-walk paths under test

## Actions
1. Add a non-trivial corpus generator: a realistic acyclic DAG (multiple
   Blocked-by edges per ticket, fan-in), a `closed/` subdirectory (recursive
   walk + folder-closure path), tags from the default vocabulary, varied
   bodies — not an identical-ticket chain.
2. Profile the corpus-heavy commands whose in-process work scales with corpus
   size (`check`, `list`, `ready`, and the dependent-scan mutators `close`,
   `rm`) across a 3-point 4× ladder {64, 256, 1024}. `next-id` is excluded: its
   dominant cost is a `git for-each-ref`/`ls-tree` subprocess invisible to
   ReadMemStats (and a no-op against the non-git temp corpus).
3. Assert linear scaling: allocation-volume (bytes) ratio between consecutive
   sizes stays below the linear/quadratic separator (4× expected linear, 16×
   quadratic), with a permanent O(N²) negative control proving the guard trips.
4. Add `make test-scaling`; keep it out of `make test` / `make unit-test`.

## Test
- `make test-scaling` runs `TestScaling*` (tag `scaling`); the five commands
  report ratios near 4.0 and the suite passes.
- `TestScalingLinearNegativeControl` runs an O(N²) probe and asserts the ratio
  exceeds the ceiling — proves the guard is not vacuous.
- `TestScalingCorpusValid` asserts the fixture has zero corpus errors.
- `make test` / `go test ./...` do NOT compile or run the tagged file
  (verify the binary/coverage run is unchanged).

## Verification
- [ ] `make test-scaling` green; ratios logged near 4.0 for every command
- [ ] Negative control trips (ratio > ceiling on the O(N²) probe)
- [ ] Fixture validates clean in code (validateCorpus → 0 errors)
- [ ] `make test` unaffected (tagged file excluded)
- [ ] Corpus is non-trivial: multi-edge DAG, closed/ subdir, tags

## Invariants
- No new dependencies; stdlib + Go toolchain only.
- Default `make test` runtime and coverage unchanged.
- The generated corpus validates clean (errors → 0; warnings allowed).

## Exit criteria
- A build-tagged scaling test exercising check/list/ready/close/rm
  on a non-trivial store, asserting linear growth, run via `make test-scaling`
  and excluded from the default suite. `make check` / `make test` green.
